**Idea.** `malloc` only guarantees alignment for the largest fundamental type (usually 16 bytes). To get alignment `A` (power of two), over-allocate, round the address up to a multiple of `A`, and stash the original pointer just before the returned address so the matching free can find it.

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void *aligned_malloc(size_t size, size_t align) {
    if (align == 0 || (align & (align - 1)) != 0) return NULL; // not power of 2
    if (align < sizeof(void *)) align = sizeof(void *);
    void *raw = malloc(size + align - 1 + sizeof(void *));
    if (!raw) return NULL;
    uintptr_t addr = ((uintptr_t)raw + sizeof(void *) + align - 1)
                     & ~(uintptr_t)(align - 1);
    ((void **)addr)[-1] = raw;     // remember the real block
    return (void *)addr;
}

void aligned_free(void *p) {
    if (p) free(((void **)p)[-1]);
}
```

**Why it works.**

- `raw + sizeof(void*)` leaves room for the saved pointer; rounding up adds at most `align - 1` bytes, so `addr + size` never exceeds the allocated block.
- `x & ~(align - 1)` clears the low bits, which rounds down; adding `align - 1` first makes it round up. This only works for power-of-two alignment, which is why we validate it.
- `addr` is at least 8-byte aligned, so the slot at `addr - sizeof(void*)` is also aligned for a pointer store.
- The returned pointer must be released with `aligned_free`, never plain `free`.

**Cost and caveats.** Wasted space is up to `align + sizeof(void*) - 1` bytes per block; for huge alignments (page size) prefer `posix_memalign` or C11 `aligned_alloc` (size must be a multiple of alignment), or `mmap` for page alignment. Check `size + align + ...` for `size_t` overflow in production code. Alignment matters for SIMD loads, DMA buffers, cache-line isolation (avoiding false sharing) and some hardware that faults on unaligned access.
